[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Expo EAS Build, Development Builds, and Bare Workflow Management in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As React Native projects scale, the standard Expo Go client often falls short due to custom native module requirements. This tutorial covers transitioning to Expo Application Services (EAS) Build, configuring custom Development Builds, and managing the Bare Workflow effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Prerequisites and EAS CLI Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, ensure you have Node.js installed. Install the global EAS CLI and log in to your Expo account to authorize builds."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"npm install -g eas-cli
eas login"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Configuring eas.json"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The eas.json file controls how your builds are generated across different profiles like development, preview, and production. Create or update this file in your project root."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"{
  "cli": {
    "version": ">= 5.0.0"
  },
  "build": {
    "development": {
      "developmentClient": true,
      "distribution": "internal"
    },
    "preview": {
      "distribution": "internal"
    },
    "production": {}
  },
  "submit": {
    "production": {}
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Generating Custom Development Builds"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Development builds replace Expo Go, allowing you to test native modules. Run the following command to build the development client for Android or iOS."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"eas build --profile development --platform android"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Development builds contain your specific native dependencies."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"They connect to your local Metro bundler via WebSocket just like Expo Go."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"You can install pre-build binaries directly onto emulators or physical devices."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Managing the Bare Workflow (Prebuild)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Expo's Continuous Native Generation (CNG) approach allows you to manage the android and ios directories dynamically using the prebuild command."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"npx expo prebuild --clean"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command generates native project files based on your app.json configurations and installed plugins. Never modify native files directly if you wish to maintain a fully managed workflow."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Executing Production Builds"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once testing is complete, use EAS Build to generate release artifacts (.aab for Google Play, .ipa for Apple App Store)."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"eas build --platform all --profile production"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"EAS handles code signing, provisioning profiles, and keystores automatically, drastically reducing deployment complexity."}]
